<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Faculty of Environment and Information Studies, Keio University, Kanagawa, Japan<span id="id9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">moz@sfc.keio.ac.jp, {shusuke.komatsu, souta.noguchi}@querylift.co.jp, ataka@sfc.keio.ac.jp</span></span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kazuto Ataka
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Faculty of Environment and Information Studies, Keio University, Kanagawa, Japan<span id="id10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">moz@sfc.keio.ac.jp, {shusuke.komatsu, souta.noguchi}@querylift.co.jp, ataka@sfc.keio.ac.jp</span></span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze answers generated by generative engines (GEs) from the perspectives of citation publishers and the content-injection barrier, defined as the difficulty for attackers to manipulate answers to user prompts by placing malicious content on the web.
GEs integrate two functions: web search and answer generation that cites web pages using large language models.
Because anyone can publish information on the web, GEs are vulnerable to poisoning attacks.
Existing studies of citation evaluation focus on how faithfully answer content reflects cited sources, leaving unexamined which web sources should be selected as citations to defend against poisoning attacks.
To fill this gap, we introduce evaluation criteria that assess poisoning threats using the citation information contained in answers.
Our criteria classify the publisher attributes of citations to estimate the content-injection barrier thereby revealing the threat of poisoning attacks in current GEs.
We conduct experiments in political domains in Japan and the United States (U.S.) using our criteria and show that citations from official party websites (primary sources) are approximately <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\%</annotation></semantics></math> in the U.S. and <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>65</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65\%</annotation></semantics></math> in Japan, indicating that U.S. political answers are at higher risk of poisoning attacks.
We also find that sources with low content-injection barriers are frequently cited yet are poorly reflected in answer content.
To mitigate this threat, we discuss how publishers of primary sources can increase exposure of their web content in answers and show that well-known techniques are limited by language differences.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language model (LLM) applications such as GPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, Gemini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, and Claude <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> increasingly incorporate web search functions, providing internet users with new ways of accessing information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
Information retrieval has become a primary use case for LLMs, with a survey in September <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2025" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2025</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2025</annotation></semantics></math> showing that about <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math> of all prompts involve information seeking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
Systems that perform web searches and generate answers are known as <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generative engines</em> (GEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
Web search services are rapidly transitioning from traditional search engines to GE-based systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, changing the way users access and interpret web contents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">GEs not only alter the delivery of information but also reshape its nature.
Whereas conventional search engines direct users to primary information sources such as official documents, GEs provide synthesized answers that summarize and interpret web content.
LLMs also exhibit bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, meaning that users receive secondary or higher-order information produced by LLMs rather than directly reading primary sources, similar to human-to-human information delivery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>.
This transformation enhances accessibility but centralizes the selection and interpretation of web content in the GE; moreover, major GEs are black-box systems, introducing vulnerabilities in factual accuracy and source reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">GEs cite content published on the web by various publishers, including attackers with malicious intent or for profit; therefore, they remain vulnerable to poisoning attacks that manipulate web content to produce attacker-intended answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
When GEs generate answers lacking factuality, the cause is not only LLM hallucinations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> but also the lack of factuality in the web content obtained through web searches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
Web content can be published and modified by any user with malicious intent or for profit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
Prior studies reveal the vulnerability of GEs to poisoning attacks that place disinformation and misinformation on the web to generate attacker-intended answers because GEs can cite such content and generate text that reflects it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>.
To increase the exposure of web content as citations, <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Generative Engine Optimization</em> (GEO) presents patterns of which styles of web content GEs prefer to cite in answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
Although GEO is beneficial for primary information providers that seek to increase exposure of their web content as citations, it can also facilitate poisoning attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Existing evaluation criteria aim to assess how faithfully the cited content is reflected in the answers generated by GEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
These studies analyze textual and semantic consistency between the cited web content and GE-generated answers.
However, semantic consistency alone does not capture the authority of the cited web sources.
Poisoning attacks exploit attacker-published secondary sources to inject incorrect content into the web, which GEs may then cite in generated answers; we therefore analyze which web sources GEs select as citations.
For information-retrieval questions, answers should ideally cite authoritative (primary) information sources relevant to the question subject.
This requirement is especially critical in the political domain, where the accurate delivery of political information to citizens is essential to safeguarding democracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.
This gap arises because prior studies focus on whether cited sources are faithfully reflected in specific sentences of generated answers, whereas we focus on the distinct issue that GEs can become vulnerable to poisoning by citing non-authoritative sources in information-retrieval questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we introduce novel evaluation criteria that focus on the publisher attributes of cited sources and their impact on answer generation.
Our approach proceeds in three steps.
First, we classify web publishers into primary and secondary information sources and further divide secondary ones into categories such as media, platform, owned, academia, and non-media industry, using a <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">LLM-as-a-Judge</em> method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> based on URL and WHOIS data.
Second, we quantify GE citation preferences by computing the proportion of each category among citations in generated answers.
Third, we measure how publisher categories affect the semantic consistency between cited content and corresponding answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We apply these evaluation criteria to information-retrieval questions in the political domain across Japan and the United States (U.S.), using <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="280" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>280</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">280</annotation></semantics></math> questions and <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}400</annotation></semantics></math> generated answers.
Our results show that citations to official party websites (primary information) constitute approximately <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>65</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65\%</annotation></semantics></math> of citations in Japan but only <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\%</annotation></semantics></math> in the U.S.
We also quantify GE susceptibility to poisoning attacks by classifying publishers by the content-injection barrier—the practical difficulty of publishing content that can be surfaced and cited—into low-, medium-, or high-barrier categories based on publisher classification.
Our analysis reveals that low-barrier sources that can be published with only registration (Reddit, X, personal blog, etc.) account for approximately <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math> of citations in answers.
We further find that citations from low-barrier sources tend to have lower semantic consistency with the actual answer content than medium- and high-barrier sources, yet they still influence GE answers despite their lower semantic consistency.
To mitigate the threat, we discuss how publishers of primary information sources can increase exposure of their web content as citations in answers.
Our analysis reveals that English content aligns with GEO patterns, whereas Japanese content does not, indicating language differences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our study makes the following contributions:
First, we propose new evaluation criteria for GE-generated answers that incorporate the publisher attributes of cited information.
Second, we find that GEs cite primary sources in <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>65</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65\%</annotation></semantics></math> of Japanese political answers and <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\%</annotation></semantics></math> of U.S. ones, while low-barrier sources tend to have weaker reflection in answer text.
Third, we discuss how primary information providers can increase exposure of their web content to mitigate poisoning attacks and differentiate between English and Japanese content.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Generative Engine</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces GE and its system model.
GEs are systems that integrate web search results from queries that provide useful information for user questions and generates answers while citing source web pages using large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
GEs receive a question from users, perform web searches, and generate answers with LLMs using the search results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Pranjal et al. formalize GEs and provide a system model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
A GE is formalized as a function <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{GE}^{model}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{GE}^{model}</annotation></semantics></math> that takes a user question <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and personalization information <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> as inputs and generates a textual answer <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{GE}^{model}:=(q_{u},P_{U})\rightarrow r" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{GE}^{model}:=(q_{u},P_{U})\rightarrow r</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">GEs consist of two components: content retrieval and answer generation.
Content retrieval collects the information necessary to generate answers from the web.
The user query <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is converted by LLMs into multiple queries <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}=\{q_{1},q_{2},\cdots,q_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}=\{q_{1},q_{2},\cdots,q_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the web search.
These queries are sent to a search engine <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SE</annotation></semantics></math>, and GEs obtain a set of web sources <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{s_{1},s_{2},\cdots,s_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{s_{1},s_{2},\cdots,s_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from the search results.
Each result set for a web search query <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> typically consists of the top <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> web sources ranked by the search engine’s metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Answer generation cites the web sources obtained in the content retrieval phase and generates answers to user queries.
Web sources <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are converted into a summary set 
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sum=\{Sum_{1},Sum_{2},\cdots,Sum_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sum=\{Sum_{1},Sum_{2},\cdots,Sum_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> that extracts and summarizes the content of web sources for generating answers to the user query <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>.
LLMs then generate the final answer <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> from the summary set <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sum" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sum</annotation></semantics></math> while citing web sources <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
The answer <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> sentences <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{l_{1},l_{2},\cdots,l_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{l_{1},l_{2},\cdots,l_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and each sentence <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a citation set <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}\subseteq S,C_{i}=\{c_{1},c_{2},\cdots,c_{l}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}\subseteq S,C_{i}=\{c_{1},c_{2},\cdots,c_{l}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although a GE ideally has one or more citations for each sentence, there are cases where <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning there are no citations for the corresponding sentence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Related Works and Motivation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces security risks and evaluation methodologies for GEs to clarify the positioning and motivation of our study.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Threats to GEs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Zou et al. reveal and formalize an attack method called <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">PoisonedRAG</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> that exploits GEs to generate attacker-intended answers by placing malicious content into external databases.
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a core component of GEs, a technology that retrieves relevant content from external databases and incorporates it into LLM answer generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
PoisonedRAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> exploits RAG by allowing attackers to inject a small volume of malicious text into external databases so that GEs produce attacker-intended answers for specific questions.
This malicious content includes text that attackers want displayed as answers to specific questions, especially false information such as “<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OpenAI’s CEO is Tim Cook.</span>”
PoisonedRAG attacks target closed-ended questions like “<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Who is the CEO of OpenAI?</span>” rather than open-ended questions like “<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What are the latest trends in AI?</span>” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To succeed in a poisoning attack, the malicious content must satisfy two conditions: Retrieval Condition and Generation Condition.
Retrieval Condition means that malicious content (such as text or HTML placed by attackers) is selected as top-<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> relevant content through retrieval for target questions.
In GEs, content satisfying Retrieval Condition appears as web pages ranked highly in search engine results.
Generation Condition means that the malicious content is used as context for generating the attackers’ intended answers in the LLM.
A related prior study proposes <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generative engine optimization</em> (GEO), a method that optimizes website text structure and content to increase the exposure of web page content as citations in answers when the website passes the Retrieval Conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
This method relates to the Generation Condition.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Citation Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An ideal GE is a system where all claimed sentences in generated answers are supported by citations (citation coverage) and all citations accurately support and faithfully reflect claims (reflection accuracy), meaning the semantic consistency between cited web content and answers generated by GEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
To analyze them, numerous studies evaluate citation coverage and reflection accuracy, specifically focusing on RAG systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A core component of the analysis is faithfulness evaluation metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite> that calculate reflection accuracy.
Faithfulness evaluation metrics are typically classified into three methods: entailment-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, similarity-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, and QA-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
Similarity-based methods are most appropriate for calculating reflection accuracy.
Similarity-based methods quantitatively measure semantic similarity between two texts and rely on neural encoder models.
Zhang et al. show that similarity-based methods achieve higher precision than entailment-based and QA-based methods in tasks evaluating consistency between long documents and their summaries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>.
This approach aligns with GE behavior of summarizing web page content retrieved from search results and generating answers with citations.
Citation evaluation applies these faithfulness evaluation methods to each sentence <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of generated answer <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> with citations <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and evaluates reflection accuracy for the entire answer <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Amin et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> also analyze which of human- and LLM-generated content is preferred by RAG; this study shows RAGs prefer human-generated content over LLM-generated content when the content includes author information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Research Motivation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing studies that focus on citation evaluation have not established answer evaluation criteria for generated answers by GEs that assume PoisonedRAG attacks.
Conventional evaluation criteria for RAG or GE analyze the structure of retrieved content from external databases such as the web and the semantic consistency between the retrieved content and the generated answer; they do not consider which publishers of web content are selected as citations and the authority of cited web content.
Amin et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> analyze citation differences based on whether content is generated by LLMs or humans, but their study does not analyze which categories of publishers GEs tend to cite in answers; that is, they do not consider differences between primary and secondary information sources.
Thus, they cannot capture the case where attackers post malicious content as secondary information sources and GEs cite them to generate attacker-intended answers, failing to capture the threat of poisoning attacks against GEs.
The malicious content includes disinformation and misinformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> optimized to satisfy the Retrieval and Generation Conditions using SEO and GEO techniques.
Because PoisonedRAG attacks become widely recognized along with the spread of GEs, an evaluation criterion that assumes such attacks is critical for making GEs safer.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces novel evaluation criteria that analyze both the selection and the reflection faithfulness of web contents of citations in answers generated by GEs, as well as methods to calculate the criteria, to reveal the vulnerability of poisoning attacks on GEs.
Our method consists of two components: (1) citation classification based on publisher attributes and (2) evaluation of citation reflection based on publisher attributes.
The first component classifies web sources cited by GEs according to publisher categories to reveal which attributes appear and are selected as citations in answers.
The second component quantitatively measures semantic consistency for each citation category to reveal how each category influences answers generated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These two components are necessary to reveal the vulnerability of poisoning attacks on GEs.
In the context of citation evaluation, prior studies confirm cases where web sources cited are explicitly indicated by labeling them as citations yet their content is not reflected in the text of generated answers, as discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Citation Evaluation ‣ 3 Related Works and Motivation ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
Our method fills this gap by combining the analysis of citation selection with the analysis of citation reflection in generated answers.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Citation Classification</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first component classifies citation web sources based on publisher attributes.
This component reveals which types of web sources GEs preferentially cite.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a classifier <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(c)</annotation></semantics></math> that assigns each citation <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to an appropriate publisher attribute category.
We divide the classification into two components: primary information identification and secondary information category classification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Primary information identification.</span>
We identify primary information sources that are directly relevant to specific domains.
First, we define the target domain set <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., for the U.S. Democratic Party, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\text{democrats.org, democrats.gov, democrats.io}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>democrats.org, democrats.gov, democrats.io</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\text{democrats.org, democrats.gov, democrats.io}\}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Let <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{c}=\text{domain}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>domain</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{c}=\text{domain}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> be a function extracting the domain from each citation <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in C</annotation></semantics></math>.
We check whether <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> includes <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is included in <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we classify the citation as a primary information source.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Secondary information category classification.</span>
We categorize secondary information into publisher attribute categories to reveal the vulnerability of poisoning attacks.
We prepare publisher attribute category set <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\{\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\cdots,\lambda_{p}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\{\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\cdots,\lambda_{p}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(c)</annotation></semantics></math> maps citation <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to category <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i};i\in\{1,2,\cdots,p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i};i\in\{1,2,\cdots,p\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In our experiments in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiment ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we use labels such as “Party”, “Media”, “Platform”, “Owned”, “Academia”, and “Non-media-industry”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To build the function <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(c)</annotation></semantics></math>, we adopt a hybrid strategy combining automatic category classification using LLM-as-a-Judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> and manual category classification.
We prepare two GE models <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{model}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>model</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{model}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{model}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>model</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{model}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (ideally two models from different providers such as GPT-5 and Gemini Pro with web search mode enabled) and use them to identify publishers from domains and WHOIS information.
Combining multiple GE models can reduce potential biases of single GE models and increase classifier accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>.
When classification results match between the two GE models, we adopt that result; when they disagree, we conduct final human judgment (in our experiments, the authors determine the final judgment from company information on the website and domain information).
To classify with GE, we show the example prompt for classification used in our experiments in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompt for Category Classification ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
Finally, we obtain the set of citations classified into each category <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\lambda_{i}}=\{c\in C\mid\lambda(c)=\lambda_{i}\};i\in\{1,2,\cdots,p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\lambda_{i}}=\{c\in C\mid\lambda(c)=\lambda_{i}\};i\in\{1,2,\cdots,p\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Citation Reflection Measurement</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The second component measures which publisher attribute categories of citation web sources are reflected faithfully in generated answers through textual and semantic similarity analysis.
Some sentences of answer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> have citation labels attached when GEs generate the sentences and content of the web sources; however, prior studies report cases where no relationship exists between citation content and sentences with citation labels.
To address this and reveal the tendency of citation reflection by publisher attribute category, we quantitatively evaluate textual and semantic similarity between citations <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in C</annotation></semantics></math> and answer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we analyze how publisher attribute categories of citation web sources are reflected in answer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and visualize the degree of influence each category has.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We decompose the measurement into four components:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decomposing answers and citations into sets of sentences.</span>
First, we decompose answer <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> into sentences <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{l_{1},l_{2},\ldots,l_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{l_{1},l_{2},\ldots,l_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and each citation <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\in C</annotation></semantics></math> into sentences <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{c_{i}}=\{s_{i,1},s_{i,2},\ldots,s_{i,n_{i}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{c_{i}}=\{s_{i,1},s_{i,2},\ldots,s_{i,n_{i}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because some sentences of answer <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> have citation labels attached and the semantics of each answer sentence is independent, we need to decompose answer <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> into sentences to evaluate similarity accurately.
To decompose answer <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and citation <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into sentences, we introduce a sentence splitting function <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SentenceSplit}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SentenceSplit</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SentenceSplit}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> which decomposes text <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> into sentences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Similarity measurement.</span>
We measure textual and semantic similarity between each answer sentence <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{l_{1},l_{2},\ldots,l_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{l_{1},l_{2},\ldots,l_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and each citation sentence <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{c_{i}}=\{s_{i,1},s_{i,2},\ldots,s_{i,n_{i}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{c_{i}}=\{s_{i,1},s_{i,2},\ldots,s_{i,n_{i}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for each citation <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\in C</annotation></semantics></math>.
To measure similarity between two sentences, we introduce the calculation of similarity function <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> between two sentences <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.
The function <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> returns a similarity score between the two sentences <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> as a value in the range of <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, a similarity value closer to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates lower semantic similarity, whereas a value closer to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates higher semantic similarity.
We categorize similarity scores into three bands to assess how strongly citation sentences are reflected in answer sentences, considering <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> as an answer sentence and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> as a citation sentence:
“High” similarity (scores in <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.9,1.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.9,1.0]</annotation></semantics></math>) indicates strong semantic alignment between answer and citation sentences, suggesting direct reflection of the citation source in the answer.
“Mid” similarity (scores in <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0.8,0.9]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0.8,0.9]</annotation></semantics></math>) represents moderate semantic overlap, indicating partial citation contribution.
“Low” similarity (scores in <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1.0,0.8]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1.0,0.8]</annotation></semantics></math>) reflects weak semantic connection, suggesting minimal reflection of the citation source in the answer.
We employ these thresholds because <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math> has been used as a threshold for detecting paraphrased sentences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9</annotation></semantics></math> for classifying similar sentences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> in previous studies.
To calculate the similarity, we use Sentence-BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> to convert each sentence into embedding vectors and calculate their semantic similarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we calculate the maximum similarity between the answer sentence set <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{l_{1},l_{2},\ldots,l_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{l_{1},l_{2},\ldots,l_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and citation <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}_{\text{max}}(S,c_{i})=\max_{j\in\{1,\ldots,k\},m\in\{1,\ldots,n_{i}\}}\text{sim}(l_{j},s_{i,m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>sim</mtext><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>sim</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}_{\text{max}}(S,c_{i})=\max_{j\in\{1,\ldots,k\},m\in\{1,\ldots,n_{i}\}}\text{sim}(l_{j},s_{i,m})</annotation></semantics></math> to reveal the reflection influence power of a citation in answers.
In calculating this maximum similarity, we compute the similarity between each answer sentence <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{j}\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{j}\in S</annotation></semantics></math> and all sentences <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i,m}</annotation></semantics></math> within citation <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and adopt the highest similarity value across all combinations.
GEs often use only some sentences within long citation texts to generate particular answer sentences.
Therefore, finding the maximum similarity identifies the most relevant part of the citation text to the answer content.
This method reveals the reflection influence power of citation sources in answer <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> without relying on citation labels attached by GEs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Citation source category classification.</span>
After obtaining maximum similarity scores <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}_{\text{max}}(S,c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>sim</mtext><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}_{\text{max}}(S,c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for each citation <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, we categorize each citation using the classifier <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This categorization allows us to examine how different publisher attribute categories are reflected faithfully in answers at varying similarity levels.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiment</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section shows citation patterns on GEs in the political domains of Japan and the U.S.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06823v1/all_parties_models_integrated_en_politics_prompts_red-blue.svg" id="S5.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/380;" width="476" height="380"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Citation Distributions (U.S.)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06823v1/all_parties_models_integrated_ja_politics_prompts_red-blue.svg" id="S5.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/190;" width="476" height="190"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Citation Distributions (Japan)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06823v1/full_legend.svg" id="S5.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/7;" width="476" height="7"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of citation sources by party in the US (a) and Japan (b): Each stacked bar shows the proportion of cited sources (primary, opponent, and secondary information sources categorized by attack cost) for responses generated by different APIs.
For each party, the three adjacent bars correspond to results from OpenAI (left), Gemini (middle), and Claude (right).
The three bars on the far right of each panel show aggregated results across all parties. The “opponent source” in All Parties chart reffers to the citation sources from parties that are not the target party for each question. The numbers under each chart shows the amout of total citation constructing it.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the method proposed in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Methodology ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we conduct experiments targeting the political domains of Japan and the U.S.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We design political questions about topics common to both countries and generate answers to those questions.
We prepare ten policy questions and ten ideology questions, totaling twenty questions.
All questions are closed-ended and include a party name.
For example:
“<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Regarding government debt, does <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">{</span>party name<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">}</span></span> currently prioritize debt restraint or growth-oriented investment?</span>”
We show all questions in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Political Question Templates ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
The questions are prepared in two languages, Japanese and English, for politics in Japan and the U.S. respectively.
Answers to Japanese questions for GEs are generated in Japanese, and answers to U.S. questions are generated in English.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We query political parties that satisfy each country’s requirements for national political parties as primary information sources.
Specifically, in Japan we target nine parties: “Liberal Democratic Party (LDP)”, “Constitutional Democratic Party of Japan (CDP)”, “Komeito”, “Japan Innovation Party (JIP)”, “Democratic Party for the People (DPP)”, “Japanese Communist Party (JCP)”, “Reiwa Shinsengumi (Reiwa)”, “Sanseito”, and “Conservative Party of Japan (CPJ)”; in the U.S. we target five parties: “Republican Party (GOP)”, “Democratic Party (DP)”, “Green Party (GPUS)”, “Libertarian Party (LP)”, and “Constitution Party (COP)”.
We prepare twenty questions for each party, resulting in <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="180" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>180</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">180</annotation></semantics></math> questions for Japan and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> questions for the U.S. respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We employ three GE models for answer generation: OpenAI GPT-5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, Claude Sonnet <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> (claude-4-sonnet-20250514) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and Gemini Flash <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.0</annotation></semantics></math> (gemini-2.0-flash) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, the most advanced publicly available models as of September <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2025" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2025</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2025</annotation></semantics></math>.
We select them because their APIs provide both search results and citation sources.
In our experiments, the temperature of each GE model cannot be set because APIs do not allow setting temperature for LLMs with search mode enabled; thus, the cited web sources and textual content of answers vary across generations.
To capture answer variation, we ask each question five times to suppress bias from a single answer.
The answers are obtained as of September <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2025" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2025</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2025</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In visualizations of citation patterns, we group citation web sources in answers by the content-injection barrier to placing malicious content intended to manipulate specific answers.
Content-injection barrier is the practical difficulty for an actor to publish content on a domain so that it can be retrieved and cited for a poisoning attack.
We use this grouping because the barrier provides an objective proxy that indirectly reflects a domain’s vulnerability to poisoning.
We group the publisher attributes of citation web sources into five categories: Primary Sources, Opponent Sources, Low-Barrier Sources, Medium-Barrier Sources, and High-Barrier Sources.
Primary Sources are domains owned by the party referenced in the question.
Opponent Sources are domains operated by rival parties.
When a citation source from another party appears when asking a question about a specific party we selected within the same country, the classifier treats it as an Opponent Source.
Low-Barrier Sources are domains where users or owners can freely publish or edit content (e.g., social-media platforms and personal domains).
Medium-Barrier Sources are domains owned by organizations or companies with editorial processes where journalism bias or interests may appear (e.g., media outlets and industry associations).
High-Barrier Sources are domains where authors are required to remain neutral and objective and manipulation is difficult (e.g., academia and government).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Results of Explicit Citation from APIs</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the above experimental setup, we collect the URLs indicated as citation web sources through API access to each GE.
We classify each citation using the method described in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Citation Classification ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and calculate the proportion of each category as <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda_{i}}=|C^{\lambda_{i}}|/|C|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda_{i}}=|C^{\lambda_{i}}|/|C|</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C^{\lambda_{i}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C^{\lambda_{i}}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of citations in category <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C|</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of citations.
We show the aggregated results in Figures <a href="#S5.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>–<a href="#S5.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>.
These figures present stacked proportions by party and model.
Each group of three stacked bar graphs shows the proportions of publisher attribute categories of citation web sources in the answer on the horizontal axis.
For each party, the stacks are ordered left to right as OpenAI, Gemini, and Claude, respectively.
The three graphs at the left edge of each figure show the combined results of all citations across parties as a baseline for each model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first analyze the overall trends in citation patterns.
Overall, for Japanese questions (Japanese parties), all three models show a high proportion of target-party primary information sources, accounting for about <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\%</annotation></semantics></math>: OpenAI <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="63.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>63.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">63.0\%</annotation></semantics></math>, Gemini <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and Claude <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast, for U.S. questions (U.S. parties), the proportion of primary information sources decreases (OpenAI <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>43.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>, Gemini <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="27.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>27.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">27.0\%</annotation></semantics></math>, Claude <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28.1\%</annotation></semantics></math>), and the proportion of secondary information sources increases.
We observe a large structural difference: primary source dependence in the Japanese setting and external source dependence in the U.S. setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We observe distinct differences in citation patterns across the three models.
Gemini consistently exhibits strong platform dependence: low-barrier sources contribute <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> in Japanese questions and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> in U.S. questions.
Claude tends to use media-related sources the most: medium-barrier sources contribute <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> in Japanese questions and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> in U.S. questions.
OpenAI cites categories in a more balanced manner: medium-barrier sources <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> and low-barrier sources <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> in Japanese questions, whereas high-barrier sources <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> and medium-barrier sources <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>23.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> in U.S. prompts.
Gemini is platform-leaning, Claude is media-leaning, and OpenAI is distributed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We identify significant differences in citation patterns between Japanese and U.S. contexts.
With Japanese questions, all three models converge to around a <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\%</annotation></semantics></math> share for primary information sources, and opponents and secondary information sources are used in a supplementary role.
With U.S. questions, the share of primary information sources falls to the <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40\%</annotation></semantics></math> range, and the shortfall is supplemented by different opponent and secondary information sources depending on the model.
Concretely, in the U.S., Gemini emphasizes platform sources (<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>), Claude emphasizes media sources (<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>), and OpenAI emphasizes high-barrier sources (<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that the direction of external dependence diverges substantially by country and language.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We examine citation patterns for individual U.S. political parties.
For both the Republican and Democratic Parties, the two major parties in the U.S., the proportion of opponents and secondary information sources is high across models, with medium- and high-barrier sources as the main components.
Except for questions about the Democratic Party with OpenAI, the two parties often cite low- and medium-barrier sources.
In contrast, the Green Party frequently cites low-barrier sources.
Other parties tend to cite official party sources more, though the volume varies across models and parties.
For the Green Party, OpenAI and Claude have high shares of primary information sources (<math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively), whereas for Gemini roughly <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> of that share is replaced by medium-barrier sources.
For the Libertarian Party and the Constitution Party, similar tendencies are observed: only OpenAI shows a remarkably high share (greater than <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math>) from primary information sources, whereas other models increase dependence on low-barrier sources to around <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze citation patterns for individual Japanese political parties.
The current ruling party, the Liberal Democratic Party, shows a mitigated concentration on primary information sources compared with other parties.
Meanwhile, citations from high-barrier sources, which are opponents and secondary information sources with high barriers, increase; nevertheless, compared with questions about other parties, citations from high-barrier opponents and secondary information sources remain relatively high.
For the Constitutional Democratic Party of Japan, Komeito, and the Japan Innovation Party, primary information sources generally account for <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and secondary information sources remain supplementary.
The Japanese Communist Party exhibits particularly high dependence on the party’s official domain, with answer justifications concentrating in intra-party information.
For Sanseito and the Conservative Party of Japan, the shares from platforms and media increase relatively in Gemini and Claude, indicating a structure in which the shortage of party primary information sources is supplemented by opponents and secondary information sources.
Looking at the results for the Liberal Democratic Party in Japan and the Republican Party and Democratic Party in the U.S., questions about the major parties in their countries tend to have low citations from primary information sources.
This could be because those parties are major parties in their countries, and there may be influence from the vast number of secondary information sources, especially in government and media domains.</p>
</div>
