</div>
<figure id="A2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06823v1/comparison_histograms_four_columns_openai.svg" id="A2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/207;" width="476" height="207"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="A2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution comparison of web structure features between Citations (left) and Sources (right) for U.S. parties (left two columns) and Japanese parties (right two columns), showing significant differences between cited and non-cited sources</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Analysis of Web Content Structure</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section analyzes structural differences between web pages cited by GEs in their answers and web pages GEs visited but did not cite, and validates whether these differences align with insights from GEO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> that exposure of web pages as citations depends on the visibility and structure of page text.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiment Setup: </span>

We limit the target model to GPT-5 because OpenAI’s API provides access to both the search result set <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and the citation set <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, enabling systematic analysis of selection patterns.
Using the results obtained from the experiments in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiment ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we collect the list of all URLs <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that the GE visited during web search execution from the search query sequence <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}=\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}=\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the GE and extract the citation source sequence <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> embedded in response <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> (where each <math id="A3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\in S</annotation></semantics></math>).
We conduct the analysis as a two-group comparison between candidates (<math id="A3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>) and citations (<math id="A3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>), ensuring sufficient sample sizes for closed-ended questions about U.S. political parties (in English) and Japanese political parties (in Japanese) (e.g., U.S. parties <math id="A3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=370" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>370</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=370</annotation></semantics></math>, Japanese parties <math id="A3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=753" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>753</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=753</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use four web structure labels as evaluation metrics.
<span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">link_count</span> represents the total number of <span id="A3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;a&gt;</span> tags within a page (including external and internal links), whereas <span id="A3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text_density</span> indicates text density per heading hierarchy (<math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{total\_chars}/\text{number\_of\_}\texttt{&lt;h2&gt;}\text{-to-}\texttt{&lt;h6&gt;}\text{\_tags}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>total_chars</mtext><mo>/</mo><mrow><mtext>number_of_</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;h2&gt;</mtext><mtext>-to-</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;h6&gt;</mtext><mtext>_tags</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{total\_chars}/\text{number\_of\_}\texttt{&lt;h2&gt;}\text{-to-}\texttt{&lt;h6&gt;}\text{\_tags}</annotation></semantics></math>).
<span id="A3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text_length</span> measures the total text volume of the entire page, and <span id="A3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ul_count</span> counts the occurrences of <span id="A3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;ul&gt;</span> tags (number of unordered list elements).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address sample size imbalance, we perform random sampling from the larger group to match the size of the smaller group, ensuring statistical validity.
We evaluate the significance of differences using two types of non-parametric tests:
(i) Mann–Whitney U (MW) test for median differences, and (ii) Kolmogorov–Smirnov (KS) test.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results: </span>

Figure <a href="#A2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Appendix B Prompt for Category Classification ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents distribution comparisons between Citations (<math id="A3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>) and Sources (<math id="A3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>) for four metrics in U.S. parties (in English) and Japanese parties (in Japanese).
Table <a href="#A3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Web Content Structure ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the statistical test results for all metrics across both settings.
Key findings align with the GEO study’s claims as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Visually inspecting the central tendencies (median and mean) in Figure <a href="#A2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Appendix B Prompt for Category Classification ‣ Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> further highlights this pattern: while the U.S. results are consistent with GEO’s insights, the Japanese results often move in the opposite direction (e.g., shorter <span id="A3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text_length</span> for citations).
For <span id="A3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">link_count</span>, cited pages tend to have more links.
Japanese shows significance in both tests, whereas U.S. shows distributional significance.
Numerous links function as gateways to source and related information, supporting evidence presentation by GEs.
For <span id="A3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ul_count</span>, cited pages exhibit more <span id="A3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;ul&gt;</span> tags.
Japanese shows robust significance across tests, whereas U.S. shows median-level effects but limited distributional differences.
<span id="A3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;ul&gt;</span> tags reorganize text into point lists, enhancing information readability and extractability, consistent with the GEO perspective.
For <span id="A3.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text_density</span>, cited pages have higher text density per heading hierarchy.
U.S. shows significance in distributional tests, whereas Japanese ranges from marginal to significant across tests.
Pages with dense text under appropriately hierarchical headings are more likely to be selected.
For <span id="A3.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text_length</span>, Japanese shows that cited pages tend to be shorter,
whereas U.S. shows limited effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, English findings are consistent with GEO, whereas Japanese often shows the opposite trend.
These trends could result from several factors, such as differences in bias between languages, linguistic properties, and cultural differences in the structure of persuasive web content.
These findings suggest the necessity of further examination of GEO methods when comparing effectiveness across multiple languages.</p>
</div>
